// NEGATIVE TEST -- `async fn` is accepted by the lexer and parser, and then
// refused by the type checker.
//
// The specification's position (async-system/async-as-effect.md) is that async
// is an inferred effect and there is no `async` keyword at all. What exists is
// the opposite: `#[token("async")]` at src/lexer/token.rs:111, an `async fn`
// whose declared return type is rewritten to `Future<T>`, and a body that then
// fails to match it. The refusal is the useful part; the keyword is the debt.
// WHICH refusal changed on branch fix/d3b-tail-if. `return 1;` below is now
// refused for carrying a VALUE at all: an async body is emitted into a poll
// function that returns an int readiness flag, so there is nowhere to put the
// value -- it was evaluated and dropped, and for a non-unit output the emitted
// C did not compile. That fires before the older `Future<Int>` mismatch. The
// manifest fingerprint moved with it; the stage and the verdict did not.

async fn g() -> i64 {
    return 1;
}

fn main() {}
